# Bans. Before boot, ban.txt gets three bans (tools/fixtures/files/bans): badsite.example (all, permanent),
# highpower.example (newbies, level 61, above anyone, and not permanent, which only a file can make it) and
# *.old.example (permit, permanent). Every scenario connection's host is "scenario", and these bans are checked as
# nanny checks them: newbies when a new name is given, permit when an old one is (the connect-time check, for all, is
# in init_descriptor, which the scenario drivers don't run). Smokegod (60) lists, refuses, lifts and adds bans;
# ban.txt is rewritten with the permanent ones, and removed when none are left. A ban with no * never matches (#65),
# so "scenario" for newbies lets Freshling in; *nario for newbies keeps Greenhorn out, though the message is sent to
# no connection (#65); *cenari* is tested the wrong way round, the host in the name (#65), so Decimus gets in; scenar*
# for permit keeps Quintus out, and *bigscenarios*, which has the host in it, keeps Seer out. Then scenar* is banned
# again, which replaces the old ban, and lifted.
@place bans ban.txt
@connect god
Smokegod
smoke123
@return
@return
scroll 0
ban
ban somewhere weird
ban *
ban highpower.example
allow
allow nowhere
allow highpower.example
allow badsite.example
@file ban.txt
allow .old.example
@file ban.txt
ban scenario newbies
@connect new1
Freshling
@as god
ban *nario newbies
@connect new2
Greenhorn
@as god
ban *cenari* permit
@connect dec
Decimus
psswrd
@return
@as god
permban scenar* permit
@file ban.txt
@connect quin
Quintus
@as god
ban *bigscenarios* permit
@connect seer
Seer
@as god
ban
ban scenar*
ban
allow scenar
ban
@file ban.txt
